## 7. Addendum, same day: what "Surviving the Singularity" means

Your question: without being too optimistic, the book has to tell and sell its
premise. What does the title actually mean?

### Measured

- The phrase "Surviving the Singularity" appears 0 times in the 35 sections. The
  book never explains its own title.
- "The Singularity" is defined, as a pull quote in Chapter 1 and again in Chapter 6
  and Appendix C: "The Singularity is the point where intelligence stops being
  scarce, and every institution quietly built on its scarcity starts failing before
  anyone has written the replacement." (`03-chapter1.md:111`)
- "Surviving" is never defined. The nearest thing is on the Part III divider:
  "Surviving this isn't about predicting the future. It's about building something
  that works whatever the forecast turns out to be."
- The optimism question is already answered, in the Preface: "Optimism here means
  wanting a better outcome enough to investigate how it might actually work. It
  doesn't mean calling every concern cowardice or every product announcement a
  breakthrough." (`01-preface.md:39`)

### The word is doing three jobs

| Meaning of "surviving" | Where it shows up |
|---|---|
| You, personally, don't get flattened | The landing page: "Most people will get steamrolled. You don't have to be one of them." Chapter 2: "Audit your own moats." |
| Survival as the things you need (food, shelter, care), which you currently rent with wages | "*produce* survival instead of *renting* it" (`02-introduction.md:121`); "use growing capability to make survival less precarious" (`01-preface.md:41`) |
| Everyone gets through, not only the prepared | "Readiness that ends at your property line has a shelf life measured in weeks" (`09-chapter7.md:185`); "The promise of enough to eat includes you" (`02-introduction.md:171`) |

The book at v0.10.4 argues the second and third, and rejects the first on its own:
"A lone household with a generator and a rifle isn't ready. It's just losing more
slowly, with better lighting." (`09-chapter7.md:185`) The landing page, in this
worktree and on `main`, sells the first.

### The premise, assembled from lines you already wrote

This is an assembly, not new copy.

1. **What is ending.** "the old deal: sell your days or don't eat" (`01-preface.md:13`)
2. **Why now.** Intelligence stops being scarce, and the institutions built on its
   scarcity start failing "before anyone has written the replacement".
3. **What surviving means.** Getting through that gap with dinner no longer hanging
   on the job. "Losing the job has changed your life. It hasn't revoked your
   permission to have one." (`02-introduction.md:55`)
4. **What it does not mean.** "Not abolished. Optional." The markets stay.
5. **Who writes the replacement.** Today's direction. Either "the few people who own
   the servers, the models, and the land" (`01-preface.md:23`), or the people who
   show up.

The part that keeps it from being too optimistic is already inside your definition:
the institutions fail *before* the replacement exists. That gap is the danger, and
"surviving" names getting through it. Nothing in the definition promises the gap
closes well. It says the outcome is open, which is the reason to get involved.

### Ruling 7

Which "surviving" does the title sell? My recommendation: keep the first meaning as
the hook, since it is why someone picks the book up, and have the book say outright
and early that it means the second and third. One paragraph in the Introduction that
uses the title phrase and defines it would do that. The landing page's "Most people
will get steamrolled. You don't have to be one of them." would then need to change
too, because it promises the thing Chapter 7 says does not work. I have not drafted
either; both are positioning, and yours.
